<a href="#faq">FAQ</a>
</div>
<h2 id="stories">This Year's Comeback Stories</h2>
<p>Daniel Jones tore his Achilles on December 7 during a game against Jacksonville and has been a full participant at Colts camp since it opened, less than eight months later. He's not alone. Green Bay tight end Tucker Kraft is working back from a torn ACL and logged his first practice since the injury. Saints defensive tackle Bryan Bresee is feared to have suffered a torn ACL of his own during camp. And 49ers receiver Ricky Pearsall will miss the entire 2026 season after undergoing knee surgery. Different injuries, same underlying question for every one of these players: what does a team actually measure before trusting a rebuilt joint or tendon in a live game?</p>
<h2 id="cleared">What "Cleared" Actually Means</h2>
<p>Being cleared to practice isn't the same as being cleared to play at full speed, and it's not the same as being fully recovered athletically. Sports medicine staffs generally use a layered return-to-play protocol, and explosive testing sits near the top of it:</p>
<div class="stat-card">
<div class="stat-icon">🏥</div>
<div class="stat-title">Return-to-Play Testing Ladder</div>
<div class="stat-sub">The Order Teams Typically Follow</div>
<ul style="margin-top: 1rem;">
<li><strong>Strength symmetry:</strong> The injured limb typically needs to reach roughly 90% of the uninjured limb's strength on squat and leg press testing before more explosive work begins.</li>
<li><strong>Vertical and broad jump testing:</strong> Single-leg hop and jump distance are compared side to side. A meaningful gap between the surgical and non-surgical leg is one of the most common reasons teams hold a player back even after strength numbers look fine.</li>
<li><strong>Sprint mechanics:</strong> Timed sprints get analyzed for stride symmetry and top-end speed compared to the player's own pre-injury baseline, not just a league-average number.</li>
<li><strong>Change-of-direction and reactive testing:</strong> Cutting, planting, and reactive agility drills come last, since they load the joint in the least predictable ways — and for skill players, they're often the true gatekeeper for a return to full contact.</li>
</ul>
</div>
<p>That's why "full participant in camp" and "will look like his old self in Week 1" are two different claims. Jones has said himself that his focus right now is "getting more explosive, getting stronger" — explicitly athletic testing language, not just pain or mobility.</p>
<div class="callout">
<strong>The Historical Pattern</strong>
  Recent Achilles comebacks at quarterback offer a caution. Aaron Rodgers and Kirk Cousins both returned from Achilles tears and saw real dips in efficiency the following season — Rodgers averaging 6.7 yards per attempt, Cousins throwing 16 interceptions before being benched. Jones, at 29, returns younger than either of them did, but he also relied on his legs more than most pocket passers, which makes his explosive testing numbers this preseason worth tracking more closely than most.
</div>
