<h2 id="position">Why the Timeline Varies So Much by Position</h2>
<p>A quarterback's return-to-play threshold looks different from a running back's or a defensive lineman's, because the explosive demands are different. Jones needs push-off power and change-of-direction ability in the pocket, not top-end sprint speed. A skill player like Pearsall needs closer to full symmetrical explosiveness before a team is comfortable exposing him to contact, which is part of why some ACL and knee recoveries end up being season-long even when the surgery itself was successful. Kraft, as a tight end who needs to both block and separate, sits somewhere in between — which is why "first practice back" is genuinely a bigger step for him than it might look from the outside.</p>
