<h2 id="takeaway">The Takeaway</h2>
<p>"Full go" at the start of camp is a real milestone, not just a talking point — but it's a milestone in a longer testing process, not the finish line. The players worth watching closest over the next few weeks aren't just the ones cleared to practice; they're the ones whose explosive numbers in camp start closing the gap with who they were before the injury.</p>
<h2 id="faq">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<div class="faq-item">
<div class="faq-q">How long does it typically take to return from a torn Achilles in the NFL?</div>
<div class="faq-a">Most players return between 9 and 12 months after surgery, though the timeline varies by position and individual recovery. Daniel Jones's return at roughly 8 months, if he plays in Week 1, would be on the faster end of typical recovery windows.</div>
</div>
<div class="faq-item">
<div class="faq-q">What's the difference between being "cleared to practice" and "cleared to play"?</div>
<div class="faq-a">Clearance to practice usually means a player has passed initial strength and range-of-motion benchmarks and can participate in non-contact or limited-contact drills. Clearance to play in a live game typically requires passing additional explosive and reactive testing, including side-to-side symmetry in jump and sprint mechanics.</div>
</div>
<div class="faq-item">
<div class="faq-q">Do all positions face the same return-to-play testing?</div>
<div class="faq-a">No. The testing framework is similar in structure — strength, then jumps, then sprints, then change of direction — but the pass/fail thresholds differ by position based on what that position actually demands athletically on the field.</div>
</div>
